# neokube.net backup {{ stamp }} (UTC)

Taken from Liferay on lud by the AAP job "Back up neokube.net (Liferay on lud)".
This copy passed its restore test: `lportal.dump` was restored into a scratch
database and held the same rows as the live one (`summary.json`).

| File | What |
|---|---|
| `lportal.dump` | The PostgreSQL database `lportal`, `pg_dump` custom format |
| `document_library.tgz` | `/opt/liferay/data/document_library`: uploaded files and pictures |
| `secrets/*.json` | The administrator and database passwords, as Kubernetes secrets |
| `release.json` | The image this data belongs to: `{{ liferay_image }}` |
| `SHA256SUMS` | Check with `sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS` before restoring |

Not in here, on purpose: the search index (a reindex rebuilds it), and the
image, chart values and site sources (in git; `deploy/lud` in the Liferay
workspace rebuilds them).

## Restoring

**The usual way:** in AAP, launch "Restore neokube.net (Liferay on lud)" and give it this folder's name,
`{{ stamp }}`. It does the steps below, saves what the site held before, rebuilds the search index and checks
the result. The steps are here for when AAP is not there or Liferay cannot start.

Commands are for a shell with `oc` pointed at the cluster, in this folder.

0. Check the files: `sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS`

1. **Only if the `liferay` namespace is gone:** put the secrets back first, then
   deploy the image named in `release.json` with `deploy/lud/deploy.sh <tag>`
   and wait for Liferay to start on its empty database.

       oc create namespace liferay
       oc apply -f secrets/liferay-database.json -f secrets/liferay-default.json

2. Put the document library back while Liferay's pod is up, then stop Liferay.

       oc cp document_library.tgz liferay/{{ liferay_pod }}:/tmp/document_library.tgz -c {{ liferay_container }}
       oc exec -n liferay {{ liferay_pod }} -c {{ liferay_container }} -- bash -c \
         'rm -rf /opt/liferay/data/document_library && tar -C /opt/liferay/data -xzf /tmp/document_library.tgz && rm /tmp/document_library.tgz'
       oc scale -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} --replicas=0
       oc wait -n liferay --for=delete pod/{{ liferay_pod }} --timeout=5m

3. Replace the database with the dump.

       oc cp lportal.dump liferay/{{ database_pod }}:/tmp/lportal.dump
       oc exec -n liferay {{ database_pod }} -- bash -c \
         'dropdb -U "$POSTGRES_USER" --if-exists "$POSTGRES_DB" && createdb -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB" && pg_restore -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d "$POSTGRES_DB" --no-owner --exit-on-error /tmp/lportal.dump && rm /tmp/lportal.dump'

4. Start Liferay.

       oc scale -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} --replicas=1
       oc rollout status -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} --timeout=30m

5. Rebuild the search index. Until it is rebuilt, the home page lists no lessons and search finds
   nothing. Either sign in as the administrator (`test@liferay.com`; the password is
   `LIFERAY_DEFAULT_PERIOD_ADMIN_PERIOD_PASSWORD` in `secrets/liferay-default.json`, base64) and run
   Control Panel > Search > Index Actions > "Reindex search indexes", or have Liferay do it at one start:

       oc set env -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} -c {{ liferay_container }} LIFERAY_INDEX_PERIOD_ON_PERIOD_STARTUP=true
       oc rollout status -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} --timeout=30m
       # when the home page lists the lessons again, take it back off (one more restart):
       oc set env -n liferay statefulset/{{ liferay_statefulset }} -c {{ liferay_container }} LIFERAY_INDEX_PERIOD_ON_PERIOD_STARTUP-

To look at the dump without touching the live database, restore it under
another name instead of step 3:

    oc cp lportal.dump liferay/{{ database_pod }}:/tmp/lportal.dump
    oc exec -n liferay {{ database_pod }} -- bash -c \
      'createdb -U "$POSTGRES_USER" restore_check && pg_restore -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d restore_check --no-owner --exit-on-error /tmp/lportal.dump'
    # ... look around with psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d restore_check, then:
    oc exec -n liferay {{ database_pod }} -- bash -c 'dropdb -U "$POSTGRES_USER" restore_check; rm /tmp/lportal.dump'
